  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "Encarte do Povo",
    "overview": "**Creative North Star: \"Encarte do Povo\"**\n\nThe site is a Brazilian supermarket flyer turned into a transparency portal. Every page sits on a flat flyer-yellow field; content lives on white tiles outlined in 2px ink; the cost of a parliamentarian is printed as a shelf price tag with superscript cents, beside the everyday things that money buys (minimum wages, food baskets, plane tickets, square metres). Every number carries a blue rubber stamp that links to the official source it came from.\n\nThe flyer gives the material, not the voice. The tag, the stamp, the shelf and the flat clip-art objects are borrowed; the promo grammar (starbursts, \"OFERTA\", \"IMPERDÍVEL\", exclamation copy) is excluded, and the copy reads as neutral portal text. The system refuses both the blue government data table and the red protest poster: red appears only inside the ✕ presence mark.\n\nDensity is mobile-first and generous: one column of tiles on a phone, a two-column hero and multi-column shelves on desktop, body text always at reading size, charts built in HTML so their labels never shrink.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Flat yellow field, white tiles, 2px ink outlines, no decorative shadows.",
      "One variable family (Archivo) on two axes: condensed 62–75% width for display, prices and labels; 100% width for body.",
      "Price tag as the largest object in its frame; cents superscript.",
      "A source stamp on every number; every R$ value also stated in minimum wages.",
      "State shown by mark shape (✓ J ✕), not by color alone."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Red Is a Mark Rule",
        "body": "Red (#c81e1e) exists only as the fill of the ✕ \"absence without justification\" mark. Never use it for text, alerts, emphasis, headlines, or chart series.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Series Stay in Charts Rule",
        "body": "The five series colors encode the four cost components and the reference, consistently on every page (salário, cota, verba, auxílio, salário mínimo). Do not reuse them as UI accents, and do not swap their order or meaning.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Ink Labels Rule",
        "body": "Chart text is always ink, never the series color.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Two Widths Rule",
        "body": "Condensed width (62–75%) is for display, prices, numerals and labels; running text is always 100% width. Never set a paragraph condensed.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Superscript Cents Rule",
        "body": "A price shown as a tag splits into R$ / integer / cents, with currency and cents raised and reduced. Inline prices in prose stay plain `R$ 46.366,19`.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tabular Rule",
        "body": "Every numeric column, chart value and table cell uses tabular numerals.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Outline Not Shadow Rule",
        "body": "A surface is separated by a 2px ink border on yellow. Tiles, cards, tags and stamps carry no shadow; only true floating overlays may.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** put content on white tiles with a 2px ink border and 10px radius over the yellow field.",
      "**Do** show a cost as a yellow price tag with superscript cents, and make it the largest object in its frame.",
      "**Do** link every number to its official source with a blue stamp or a \"fonte\" link, and label its evidence state.",
      "**Do** state every R$ value also in minimum wages.",
